          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    
    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Uneven skin tone and facial pigmentation develop when skin cells overproduce melanin in response to sunlight, breakout inflammation, hormonal fluctuations, or a damaged barrier.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Facial pigmentation happens when melanocytes produce excess melanin triggered by ultraviolet and visible light, post-acne inflammation, hormonal shifts, or chronic barrier irritation. While surface marks usually respond well to daily sunscreen and gentle brightening topicals over eight to twelve weeks, deeper dermal pigment or melasma requires long-term management and may need clinical care from a dermatologist. The most effective starting step is wearing broad-spectrum sunscreen every morning to prevent light exposure from darkening existing spots.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Dark marks after a breakout, shadowy patches across the upper lip, or blotchy skin after sun exposure are remarkably common. Skin colour comes from melanin, a natural pigment made by melanocytes in the lower epidermis to shield cells from environmental damage.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When melanocytes face stress, injury, or hormonal shifts, they deposit surplus pigment unevenly into neighbouring cells.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35151757/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">1</a></sup> Because Indian skin has higher baseline melanin, these cells react quickly to irritation. A pimple that clears in days can leave a dark mark that lasts for months. Pinpointing the trigger makes it simpler to clear discolouration without causing further irritation.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The four main triggers of facial pigmentation
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Facial discolouration is driven by several distinct biological triggers:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Sunlight and visible light.</strong> Ultraviolet rays stimulate melanin synthesis, while high-energy visible light (blue light from sunlight) penetrates even deeper into the skin. In deeper complexions, visible light triggers intense, long-lasting hyperpigmentation that persists for months.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">2</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH).</strong> Breakouts, bug bites, and aggressive scrubbing release inflammatory messengers that instruct melanocytes to overproduce melanin as skin heals.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20725554/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">3</a></sup> Picking or squeezing blemishes worsens this inflammation, pushing pigment deeper into the skin.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hormonal shifts and melasma.</strong> Melasma appears as symmetrical brownish patches across the cheeks, forehead, and upper lip. It is strongly linked to oestrogen and progesterone changes during pregnancy or hormonal medication, and ambient heat readily triggers flare-ups.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28726212/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">4</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Barrier disruption and chronic friction.</strong> Over-cleansing with harsh face washes, rough scrubbing, or overusing strong acids strips your moisture barrier. The resulting subclinical inflammation prompts protective melanin, driving the shadowy darkening frequently seen around the mouth and chin.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42109558/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">5</a></sup>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Identifying your primary trigger ensures you focus on the right solution, whether that means calming inflammation, repairing your barrier, or increasing sun protection.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why Indian skin develops deeper, more stubborn discolouration
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Dermatologists classify skin tones using the Fitzpatrick scale, where South Asian complexions typically fall into types IV to VI. While higher melanin levels provide built-in defense against sunburn, they also make pigment-producing cells far more reactive to low-level irritation.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Melanocytes in Indian skin produce more eumelanin, the darker brown-black pigment. When a breakout clears on fair skin, it often leaves temporary redness. On Indian skin, that same blemish almost always produces brown or slate-grey hyperpigmentation.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Pigment depth also dictates recovery time. Epidermal pigment sheds naturally as surface cells renew every four to six weeks. But when inflammation is severe or a spot is picked, melanin drops into the deeper dermis, where immune cells trap it. Dermal pigment takes six to twelve months of patient, barrier-first care to fade.
    </p>

    
