    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Key topical ingredients that help even out skin tone
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Fading uneven pigmentation effectively requires a multi-pathway strategy. Relying on a single active rarely resolves the issue because melanin production is an interconnected chain of cellular events:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Inhibiting tyrosinase.</strong> Tyrosinase is the enzyme needed to produce melanin. Kojic acid binds copper ions in tyrosinase, slowing new pigment production at the source.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29552273/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">6</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Interrupting melanosome transfer.</strong> Once melanin is made, it travels into surface cells inside parcels called melanosomes. Niacinamide interrupts this transfer, helping skin look more uniform without bleaching your natural tone.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12100180/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">7</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Encouraging gentle surface renewal.</strong> Bio-retinoids promote cell turnover, helping skin shed pigmented surface cells. They refine texture and visible pores without the peeling or sun sensitivity linked to traditional retinol.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Neutralising oxidative stress.</strong> Antioxidants like vitamin C and green tea leaf extract neutralise free radicals from ultraviolet rays and pollution, halting signals that trigger pigment cells.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Supporting the barrier.</strong> Combining brightening actives with five ceramides, cholesterol, and Centella asiatica keeps skin calm, preventing the irritation that prompts rebound hyperpigmentation.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Formulas like <a href="/products/dark-spots-pore-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Dark Spots, Pore Control Serum</a> combine kojic acid, niacinamide, bio-retinoids, five ceramides, and conditioned media into a single active step.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        In the serum's own clinical study on 36 Indian women aged 24 to 44 with twice-daily use, 100% of participants improved dark spots over eight weeks. The study also recorded visible pores down 19.3% at four weeks, hydration up 254% at two weeks, and collagen up 132.4% at eight weeks. Results may vary.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Harsh scrubs, strong peels, and acid stacking trigger protective inflammation, prompting Indian skin to produce more pigment. A gentle, barrier-first routine consistently fades dark spots faster than aggressive treatments.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to build a daily routine for uneven pigmentation
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When treating uneven skin tone, simplicity outperforms complicated routines. Layering multiple serums increases irritation risk. A streamlined four-step regimen provides the best results:
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse gently.</strong> Wash with a mild, non-stripping cleanser. Avoid hot water and rough face brushes that cause friction darkening. Pat dry with a soft towel.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply active serum.</strong> Smooth three to four drops onto damp skin and let it absorb for thirty seconds as your single active step.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Moisturise.</strong> Use a lightweight moisturiser with ceramides or glycerin to seal in hydration and reinforce the barrier.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Protect every morning.</strong> Finish with a broad-spectrum SPF 50 sunscreen. Tinted mineral formulas with iron oxides provide valuable protection against visible light.</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If active breakouts lead your concerns, start with <a href="/products/acne-blemish-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Acne, Blemish Control Serum</a> to clear pores before addressing older marks. Once breakouts settle and dark spots or sun damage lead, use Dark Spots, Pore Control Serum.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Patch test on your jawline or forearm before first use. If pregnant or breastfeeding, consult your doctor; while the dark spots formula contains no retinol and no salicylic acid, medical guidance is best.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Comparing types of facial pigmentation
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Because different pigment conditions stem from different internal and external causes, their management plans differ:
    </p>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
            <thead>
                <tr style="background:#FFFEF3;">
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Pigmentation Type</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Typical Appearance and Triggers</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Best Practical Approach</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Post-Acne Marks (PIH)</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Flat brown, reddish, or purple marks located exactly where pimples or injuries healed.</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Calm active breakouts first. Use kojic acid, niacinamide, barrier repair, and daily sun protection.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Sun Spots (Solar Lentigines)</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Distinct, small, light-to-dark brown circular spots across cheekbones, temples, and forehead.</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Rigorous broad-spectrum sunscreen every day, combined with antioxidants and cellular renewal actives.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Hormonal Melasma</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Diffuse, symmetrical grey-brown patches on cheeks, upper lip, and forehead; flares with heat and UV.</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Tinted mineral sunscreen, gentle non-irritating brighteners, avoiding heat exposure, and dermatologist guidance.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;font-weight:700;">Perioral Pigmentation</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Shadowed discolouration around mouth corners and chin, often worsened by dry skin and friction.</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Stop harsh lip scrubbing and drying toothpastes. Apply ceramide-rich creams and gentle brightening serums.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Realistic timelines and when to consult a dermatologist
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Skin pigment changes take time to clear. Epidermal turnover takes roughly 28 to 40 days, so existing pigment must migrate to the surface and shed while new melanin is suppressed. Visible improvement typically requires eight to twelve weeks of consistent care.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Avoid quick-fix bleaching or steroid creams. While they may temporarily lighten skin by thinning it, they frequently trigger severe rebound hyperpigmentation and long-term barrier destruction.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        While surface discolouration responds well to consistent home care, consult a board-certified dermatologist if you notice:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Irregular spots.</strong> Any dark mark with asymmetric borders, varied colours, rapid enlargement, bleeding, or itching.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Sudden widespread darkening.</strong> Rapid pigmentation across your face or body unrelated to sun exposure or acne.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Resistant melasma.</strong> Deep dermal pigment showing no change after twelve weeks of diligent sun protection and gentle care.</li>
    </ul>

    
    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>

    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can sunscreen alone fade existing dark spots?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Sunscreen prevents spots from worsening and stops new pigment, but fading existing marks requires actives that support cell renewal and inhibit tyrosinase.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long do post-acne marks take to fade?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Superficial marks in the epidermis typically fade within two to three months of consistent care, while deeper dermal pigment can take six to twelve months.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Why does pigmentation appear darker around the mouth?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">The mouth area experiences constant movement, friction, and dryness from toothpastes, making it vulnerable to barrier irritation and protective melanin buildup.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is facial pigmentation permanent?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Most surface pigmentation fades with sun protection and gentle brighteners. Hormonal melasma can recur with sun or heat, requiring ongoing maintenance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use a brightening serum in the morning?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. Formulas with stable actives like niacinamide, kojic acid, and bio-retinoids work well in the morning when followed by broad-spectrum sunscreen.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does drinking water clear uneven skin tone?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Hydration supports skin health, but drinking water cannot dissolve melanin deposits or reverse pigmentation caused by UV exposure, acne, or hormones.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I layer multiple brightening serums for faster results?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Stacking active serums increases barrier irritation, which triggers rebound hyperpigmentation in melanin-rich skin.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    
